In order to start linear regression you need a set of data, each with one or more features that can be used to predict the target value. Linear regression is used to decide how important each feature is, giving a weight to each feature. This weight can be positive meaning that when the value a feature increases the target value usually increases. The weight can also be negative meaning when the value of a feature increases the target value usually decreases. Linear regression also sets a base value called the bias, which is the value that would be predicted when all of the features are 0. Linear regression finds the ideal weight and biases by starting off with 0 for all the weights and the bias, figuring out for each weight and bias if increasing them will make the line fit the data better, then increasing them if they will, and decreasing them if increasing them will make the line worse. This process is then repeated many times until changing the weights and biases won’t help anymore. The resulting model can then be used to predict the target value given the necessary features.
